---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "nameservers",
 "name": "Cloudflare",
 "title": "How to Change Nameservers in Cloudflare",
 "description": "Change nameservers in Cloudflare step by step: where the setting lives, what values to enter, and the propagation window to expect.",
 "intro": "Nameserver changes are delegation changes: you're telling the internet which servers hold your domain's records. Here is exactly where Cloudflare keeps that setting and what to expect.",
 "panel": "Cloudflare assigns your personal nameserver pair when the domain is added (shown on the Cloudflare DNS overview page). You don't change nameservers IN Cloudflare; you point your registrar at Cloudflare's pair.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Get your assigned pair",
   "Cloudflare dashboard, your domain, Overview: note the two nameservers (like ada.ns.cloudflare.com)."
  ],
  [
   "Change them at the registrar",
   "Wherever the domain is registered (Namecheap, GoDaddy...), set Custom nameservers to Cloudflare's pair."
  ],
  [
   "Wait for activation",
   "Cloudflare marks the domain Active when the registry delegation updates, usually 15-30 minutes, up to 24 hours."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "Cloudflare proxies (orange-clouds) most records by default. That's great for websites and terrible for mail: an MX or SPF-related record that must stay DNS-only will break email if proxied.",
 "faqs": [
  [
   "Does Cloudflare charge for DNS?",
   "No: the DNS plan is free for standard record counts, generous for any normal site."
  ],
  [
   "Can I proxy MX records?",
   "No, mail-related records must stay DNS only. Proxied mail is the most common Cloudflare misconfiguration."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in Cloudflare?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find Cloudflare's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for Cloudflare to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in Cloudflare?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in Cloudflare?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in Cloudflare, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
